  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 259 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nIsolated to scattered showers continue across the forecast area as a \nmid-level wave works through the region. Recent regional radar \ncomposite imagery shows bands of showers creeping northeastward from \nnortheast Oklahoma and southwest Kansas. These showers will renew \nrain chances into the evening hours as the mid-level disturbance \nrolls through the Plains/Midwest. Not much of anything to fear, \nhowever, as rain is expected to remain light. Little to no \ninstability should keep lightning at bay, but the possibility cannot \nbe entirely ruled out. PoPs sit between 15% and 40% across the \nregion, peaking during the afternoon through dusk timeframe. \nOvernight, a shortwave ridge will build into the Plains, keeping \nSunday dry.\n\nMonday continues to capture our attention. An amplifying upper \ntrough is expected to progress eastward into the central CONUS and \ninduce surface cyclogenesis. Ahead of this system, deep-layer \nmoisture advection is expected with strong southerly/southwesterly \nflow from the surface up to practically the tropopause. Forecast \nPWATs continue to approach or exceed the NAEFS climatological \nmaximum with some forecast soundings suggesting the potential for \nPWATs >1.9\". WPC has maintained the Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 4) for \nexcessive rainfall on Monday. Despite the highly anomalous moisture \nprofiles for this time of year, antecedent soil conditions remain \nnear normal and should hopefully help limit potential flooding \nimpacts. Shear profiles are also rather notable with potential for \ndeep-layer shear to exceed 40-50 kts. Instability is less impressive \n(on the order of a few hundred J/kg of CAPE) but would surely be \nsufficient in such a highly kinematic environment. LREF hodograph \nplumes for Monday evening have a clear preference for a deep veering \nwind profile with mean 0-3km SRH >250 m2/s2 with the 10th percentile \n0-3km SRH around 180 m2/s2. While damaging winds appear to be the \nmain concern for our area, there looks to be support for a tornado \nthreat at least some point during the evening. How much this threat \nis realized seems dependent on the timing of storms as shear \nprofiles become less supportive as the evening progresses. Improved \ntemporal resolution of guidance in forthcoming shifts should help \nclarify the evolution of the environment on Monday. The current, \ngeneral expectation for events on Monday is for convective \ninitiation to occur further west before growing upscale, leading to \na more linear storm mode locally. SPC has expanded the Marginal Risk \n(Level 1 of 5) for severe weather to include more of northwestern \nMissouri; eastern Kansas and western Missouri still remain in this \nrisk category as well. A Slight Risk (Level 2 of 5) for severe \nweather clips Linn County, KS.\n\nSome rain and storms may bleed into the day on Tuesday but are \ncurrently expected to clear by the evening hours. Behind this \nsystem, cooler temperatures settle in for the mid/late week with \nhigh temperatures generally in the low to mid 60s.",
